C programming:
	
	Searching tokenizing and anlyzing strings:
		
		Searching a string:
			string.h file has functions to finding a single charachter or substring using
				strchr():
					first argument is the string to be searched, second argument is the charachter to be searched.
					it will return a pointer to the first position in the string where the character is found.
					it is returned as pointer to charachter
					If charachter is not found, it will return NULL pointer.
					code: 
						    char str[]="the quick brown fox";   // the string to be searched
							char ch = 'q';                      //the charachter to be searched in string
							char *pGot_chr = NULL;              //pointer initialized to NULL
							pGot_chr = strchr(str,ch);         //stores address where ch is found

				strstr():
					similar to above, but searches the the instance of a substring within the given string.
					first argument is the string to be searched, second argument is the substring to be searched.
					code:
						char str[]="the quick brown fox";   // the string to be searched
						char word[] = "quick";              //the substring to be searched in string
						char *pGot_chr = NULL;              //pointer initialized to NULL
						pGot_chr = strstr(str,word);         //stores address where ch is found

		Tokenizing a string:
			a token is sequence of characters within a string that is bound by a delimiter(space, comma, period, etc)
			breaking a sentences into words is called tokenizing
			It requires 2 arguments, one is the string to be tokenized, and two is the string containing all the possible delimiter chars
				strtok():
				code:
					char str[80]="Hello how are you - my name is - jason"; 
					const char s[2] = "-";             
					char *token;             
					/*get the first token*/
					token = strtok(str,s);
					/*walk through other tokens*/
					while(token!=NULL){
						printf("%s\n", token);
						token = strtok(NULL, s);
					}
				
